414	/// A candidate must hold on the weight instrument AND still close on the
415	/// tri-ancestral instrument. These are two different questions
416	/// (`law_an_instrument_is_a_question`): `banked_walk` asks whether weight
417	/// cleared in the open, `tri_ancestral_word_verdict` asks whether the
418	/// fork/fuse pairing itself still closes over real work. A candidate that
419	/// fixes the first can break the second -- concretely, inserting an
420	/// unpaired `∈` or `∋` fixes banked exposure by giving the walk somewhere
421	/// new to deposit, while leaving a fork or fuse dangling, which flips the
422	/// tri-ancestral verdict from T to B or F. `insert_report`/`repair_count`
423	/// used to check only `banked_walk`, and a candidate that broke tri-ancestral
424	/// closure this way still printed as "holds" -- caught live, not by
425	/// inspection, checking a real repair candidate against both instruments
426	/// before building anything on it.
427	pub fn candidate_holds(cand: &str) -> bool {
428	    match banked_walk(cand) {
429	        Some(b) if b.holds() => {}
430	        _ => return false,
431	    }
432	    tri_ancestral_word_verdict(cand) == Some('T')
433	}
434	
435	/// Every single-glyph insertion that turns an exposed word into one that
436	/// both holds (banked) and still closes (tri-ancestral T). A word that
437	/// loses weight is not usually rewritten; it is repaired, and the repair
